new file mode 100644
index 0000000..74686b4
--- /dev/null
+++ b/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,32 @@
+# Daily Grind WebMCP
+
+The homepage loads `webmcp.js` as a deferred client-side script. It registers tools
+with `document.modelContext.registerTool`, falling back to the early-preview
+`navigator.modelContext` surface when present. Native WebMCP requires a supporting
+browser and a secure context (HTTPS or localhost). Unsupported browsers still have
+working order buttons; no polyfill or backend MCP transport is used.
+
+| Tool | Arguments | Result / page effect |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `get_seasonal_menu` | Optional `query` text | Returns matching drink IDs, names, descriptions and details links. Empty input returns all seasonal drinks without changing the UI. |
+| `order_drink` | Required `drinkId`; optional `quantity` (default 1, integer 1–10) | Adds to the visible local order and announces the change. Maximum 10 of each drink per order. |
+| `highlight_drink` | Required `drinkId` | Highlights, focuses and scrolls the drink card into view, and announces its details. |
+
+Drink IDs: `maple-oat-latte`, `dark-roast-espresso`, `cold-brew`.
+Menu data comes from the displayed cards. Every tool validates arguments at runtime
+and returns actionable errors without changing the page on invalid input.
+
+For example, an agent can call `get_seasonal_menu` with `{"query":"oat"}`, then
+`highlight_drink` with `{"drinkId":"maple-oat-latte"}` or `order_drink` with
+`{"drinkId":"maple-oat-latte","quantity":2}`.
+
+Orders exist only in the current page's memory and reset on reload. They do not
+submit purchases, process payments, or contact a service. The Clear order button
+resets the order. The existing details links and navigation stay available.
+Tool registrations are aborted on page exit and restored on back/forward cache
+return. Registration failure leaves the regular page controls usable.
+
+Run `npm test` for Node-based tests using a small DOM/API mock. They cover tool
+registration, menu queries, order state, validation, UI updates, unsupported
+browsers, registration failure and page lifecycle. They do not launch a browser or
+server and do not validate native browser WebMCP behavior.
